# Copy to .env.local for local dev.

# THE DATABASE. Leave this unset for local dev and you get `file:./dev.db`,
# the SQLite file prisma7.config.ts falls back to. Set it to point somewhere
# else — another file, or a libsql database over the network.
#
# `npm run dev` CHECKS IT BEFORE STARTING (ugcportal-w7wc). The `dev` script
# runs scripts/check-migrations.mjs before `next dev`; it runs `prisma
# migrate status` and then:
#
#  - nothing pending: starts the dev server, silently;
#  - pending, and DATABASE_URL is a local `file:` database (including the
#    unset default): applies them with `prisma migrate deploy` first, naming
#    each one. A file that does not exist yet is created the same way;
#  - pending, and DATABASE_URL is anything else: prints the pending
#    migrations and `npx prisma migrate deploy`, and REFUSES TO START. The
#    guard is the URL and not the database's history — a local `file:` URL
#    is written to whether or not this check created the file;
#  - the database does not exist (Prisma's P1003): created and migrated for
#    a local `file:` URL, refused the same way for anything else, with "does
#    not exist" in place of the list of names;
#  - `migrate status` failed for any other reason — drift, a half-applied
#    migration, a file it cannot read: prints Prisma's own output and
#    refuses to start. It does not try to repair anything.
#
# Those branches, with their exit codes and whether each names a command to
# run, are the table in scripts/check-migrations.test.mjs, "every branch, by
# exit code and message".
#
# It never runs `prisma migrate reset` or `prisma db push`, and a failed
# `migrate deploy` stops the dev server rather than being ignored.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS, because "dev refused to start" is annoying and the thing it
# replaces was worse: the generated Prisma client selects every column the
# schema declares, so a single unapplied migration fails every query on that
# table. In practice that is a site that renders signed out on every page and
# answers every sign-in with "Sign in misconfigured" — while still returning
# 200, because a failed session read degrades to signed-out (ugcportal-8df3).
# Nothing in the symptom points at the database. If it happens anyway (a
# server that was already running, a remote database), the server log now
# names the missing column and the migration that adds it — for the queries
# the auth adapter makes, which is where it wraps (src/lib/auth.ts); a query
# elsewhere still fails with Prisma's own message.
#
# DATABASE_URL=file:./dev.db

#
# Auth.js (next-auth). Generate a secret with `npx auth secret`.
# AUTH_URL should point at your local dev server so the OAuth callbacks
# match the redirect URIs registered in the Google/Facebook apps. Production
# redirect URIs are tracked separately (see ugcportal-5jd).
AUTH_SECRET=changeme-generate-a-real-secret-for-local-dev
AUTH_URL=http://localhost:3000
# Google OAuth client credentials (console.cloud.google.com). Add
# http://localhost:3000/api/auth/callback/google as an authorized redirect
# URI on the OAuth client for local dev.
AUTH_GOOGLE_ID=
AUTH_GOOGLE_SECRET=
# Facebook app credentials (developers.facebook.com). Add
# http://localhost:3000/api/auth/callback/facebook as a valid OAuth
# redirect URI in the Facebook app's dev/localhost settings.
AUTH_FACEBOOK_ID=
AUTH_FACEBOOK_SECRET=

# BEHIND A TLS-INTERCEPTING PROXY? (ugcportal-drt1)
#
# DO NOT set NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS in this file. It will look like it worked
# and will not work. Node reads that variable once, at process start, when
# it builds its TLS trust store; Next loads this file with dotenv from
# inside the already-running process, far too late. The value then appears
# in `process.env` while TLS never sees it.
#
# The symptom is both Google and Facebook sign-in failing with the page
# "Sign-in is misconfigured", and `SELF_SIGNED_CERT_IN_CHAIN` in the server
# log under `[auth][details]`. Note the proxy usually intercepts per host —
# accounts.google.com and *.facebook.com fail while *.googleapis.com does
# not — so a partial check can wrongly clear one provider.
#
# Instead, drop the CA PEM into `certs/` (gitignored) and `npm run dev`
# picks it up: scripts/with-local-ca.mjs sets NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS in the
# real environment of the child process before Node starts. Several PEMs in
# that directory are concatenated. With no `certs/` directory the wrapper
# does nothing at all, which is the right behaviour off the proxy.
#
# `npm run dev:inspect` (ugcportal-ymp4) deliberately does NOT go through
# that wrapper — it exists so the debugger attaches to Next rather than to
# the wrapper — so behind the proxy it reproduces the sign-in failure above.
# Export NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS in your shell if you need both at once.
#
# Exporting NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS in your shell still works and takes
# precedence; the wrapper only resolves it to an absolute path.

# WHO MAY SIGN IN (ugcportal-egp). Comma-separated email addresses.
#
# NOT THE ONLY SOURCE ANY MORE, and usually not the one to edit
# (ugcportal-t33p). The people this instance is FOR live in
# src/config/users.ts — a committed TypeScript array of
# { name, identities: ["google:addr", "facebook:addr"] } — and every identity
# listed there may sign in, in addition to whatever this variable says. That
# is also the only way to give one person two sign-in identities that share a
# single user, a single role and a single upload history; this variable gives
# each address its own user, which is Auth.js's own behaviour. Add a PERSON
# there; use this variable for an address that is not (yet) a person — a
# colleague who only needs to upload.
#
# Sign-in is refused by default. Leave this empty, ADMIN_BOOTSTRAP_EMAILS
# below empty too, and src/config/users.ts with nobody USABLE in it, and
# NOBODY can sign in — not an error, the deliberate default. The server says
# so loudly at startup, and every refused attempt is logged with the reason.
#
# "Nobody usable" rather than "empty" on purpose: what counts towards being
# configured is the array as the boot check finds it, so an array full of
# people who are all reported as mistakes counts for nothing and you get the
# same NOBODY warning. Those mistakes — an identity or an address listed
# under two people, an unknown provider, a person with no identities, a
# malformed address, a name that yields no handle or collides with another's
# — are reported separately, one line each, because they have a different
# fix from an unset variable. See docs/access-control.md for the full table.
#
# Why it exists: Google and Facebook are both configured as providers, so
# before this variable, "signed in" meant "has a Google or Facebook account",
# which is everyone. Every gate downstream — POST /api/media, /upload,
# publishing to the public gallery — asks only whether you are signed in, so
# an unrestricted sign-in made this an open public file host.
#
# The mechanism is PROVISIONAL. An allowlist is the smallest rule that is
# correct under every eventual answer (invites, a domain rule, manual
# approval); it is not a decision that an allowlist is the right one. See
# docs/access-control.md before adding any surface that depends on it.
#
# How entries are read:
#  - split on commas, trimmed, compared case-insensitively;
#  - each entry must be one exact address. There is no wildcard: a `*` entry
#    is rejected and named in the log rather than treated as "anyone", so
#    `*@example.com` permits nobody;
#  - an entry may be BOUND TO ONE PROVIDER with a `google:` or `facebook:`
#    prefix (ugcportal-1551): `google:me@gmail.com` permits that address only
#    when Google is the provider vouching for it, and the same address
#    arriving through Facebook is refused. A bare entry keeps the original
#    meaning — permitted through either provider. Any other prefix (a typo,
#    a provider that is not configured) makes the entry unusable, reported
#    like a wildcard, rather than quietly widening or narrowing it;
#  - an unusable entry is ignored, reported at startup and on every refusal,
#    and the rest of the list still applies;
#  - the address judged is the one the provider vouches for on THIS sign-in,
#    not the one stored when the account was created. A Google profile that
#    says email_verified: false is refused.
#
# IF YOU CHANGE YOUR GOOGLE OR FACEBOOK ADDRESS, list the new one here. You
# are judged on the new address from the next sign-in onwards, and you do not
# have to keep the old one listed — but note that dropping the old one ends
# any session you opened BEFORE the change, because a session is re-judged on
# the address recorded when it was minted. Sign in again and the fresh
# address decides, for that session and every later one. (Auth.js links the
# account by the
# provider's stable id, not by email, and never updates the stored address —
# so the old one lingers in the database forever and must not be what decides
# whether you can get in. See docs/access-control.md.)
#
# Add yourself here before your first deploy, or you will be locked out of
# your own instance.
#
# REMOVING AN ENTRY HERE LOGS THAT PERSON OUT (ugcportal-mzr). The rule is
# re-checked on every request, not only when someone signs IN. Each session
# carries the identity that minted it — which provider, and the address that
# provider asserted — and is judged on that, so an address you remove, or
# rebind to the other provider, loses its sessions as each one makes its next
# request, and the session row is deleted with it. Sessions that still match
# an entry you kept are left alone, including other devices of the same
# person. There is no 30-day wait and nothing to delete by hand.
#
# RESTART OR REDEPLOY AFTER EDITING. The server reads this variable from its
# own environment on every check, so there is no cache to clear — but a
# process that is already running still has the old value.
#
# What revoking does NOT do: it does not delete their account, their uploads,
# or anything they already published, and it is silent — they simply stop
# being signed in (the server logs each refusal). It also cannot tell you who
# got in while the door was open: on the deploy that first carries this gate,
# audit that first, because revoking also deletes the sessions that are the
# evidence. The commands, and what NOT to delete, are in
# docs/access-control.md under "Revoking access takes effect on the next
# request" and "What the first deploy of the sign-in gate still needs".
ALLOWED_SIGNIN_EMAILS=

# First-admin bootstrap (ugcportal-lu7). Comma-separated emails; the same
# `google:`/`facebook:` prefix as ALLOWED_SIGNIN_EMAILS is accepted, and binds
# both the sign-in it grants and the promotion: `google:me@x.com` promotes a
# Google sign-in by that address, never a Facebook one. On a fresh
# deployment every user signs up as USER and nobody can reach the admin
# screens, so this is the one path that can create an admin without one:
# when a listed user signs in, they are promoted to ADMIN and the change is
# recorded in the RoleChange audit trail. Thereafter use the admin screen at
# /admin/settings/users to grant or revoke the role.
#
# Known limits, in order of how likely they are to bite you:
#  - It grants the FIRST admin; it does not hold anyone at ADMIN. A user who
#    has ever had their role changed (by this variable or by the admin
#    screen) is skipped, so demoting someone through the UI is not silently
#    undone at their next login.
#  - It matches the email STORED ON THE USER ROW, which is not necessarily
#    the one the provider asserts today. Auth.js writes that row once, when
#    the account is first linked, and never updates it — so if you changed
#    your Google address after first signing in, list the address you had
#    BACK THEN. Listing only the new one signs you in fine (the allowlist
#    above judges the fresh address) and then silently never promotes you,
#    with nothing logged. On a brand-new account, where nothing is stored
#    yet, the two are the same address and this does not arise.
#  - Never list an address someone else could register at the provider.
#  - Anyone who can set environment variables on the deployment can make
#    themselves an admin with it. Empty it once you have your first admin —
#    but make sure the same address is in ALLOWED_SIGNIN_EMAILS first, or
#    emptying this locks you out (see the next point).
#
# How it composes with ALLOWED_SIGNIN_EMAILS above (ugcportal-egp): the two
# lists — and src/config/users.ts — are unioned into one permitted set, so an
# address listed here is also permitted to sign in and a fresh deployment that
# sets only this variable still works. That is not a way around the allowlist
# — being listed here IS a grant of access, made by whoever set the variable.
# Nothing anywhere grants access because the database is empty; the promotion
# asks whether THIS address is listed and has no role history, never whether
# there are users.
#
# BOTH VARIABLES NOW JUDGE THE SAME ADDRESS: the one your provider asserts on
# the sign-in actually happening. That used not to be true — promotion was
# judged on the address stored on your row, which diverges from the asserted
# one the moment you change your provider email, and (since ugcportal-t33p,
# where one person can hold several identities on one row) is simply whichever
# of their identities signed in first. Either way an operator could list a
# perfectly correct address here and watch nothing happen.
#
# So name the identity the person will ARRIVE with. `google:me@x.com` promotes
# a Google sign-in by that address whatever the stored one says, and there is
# no reason to list both of somebody's addresses. Nothing else about the
# promotion changed: still a listed address, still no role history, still only
# through the provider a bound entry names.
ADMIN_BOOTSTRAP_EMAILS=

# S3-compatible object storage.
# Local dev: MinIO via `docker compose up -d` (see docker-compose.yml).
# Production: DreamObjects (see ugcportal-odx) — swap these values, no code changes needed.

S3_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:9000
# Host ports docker-compose.yml publishes MinIO on (API, then console). Leave
# unset for 9000/9001. Override when 9000 is taken on your machine -- Zscaler
# holds it, for one (ugcportal-20bb) -- and change S3_ENDPOINT to match. These
# are read by `docker compose`, not by the app: compose reads `.env` by
# default, so either put them there or run
# `docker compose --env-file .env.local up -d`.
# MINIO_HOST_PORT=19000
# MINIO_CONSOLE_HOST_PORT=19001
S3_REGION=us-east-1
S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=minioadmin
S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=minioadmin
S3_BUCKET_NAME=ugcportal-dev
# MinIO requires path-style addressing; DreamObjects supports virtual-hosted style too,
# but path-style also works there, so this can usually stay "true" in every environment.
S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=true

# Server-side encryption for resale-rights evidence (ugcportal-0ss): signed
# contracts, model releases and filled-in checklists, stored under
# rights-evidence/. These are contracts and personal data, so they should be
# encrypted at rest.
#
# Left unset for local dev on purpose: the MinIO in docker-compose.yml has no
# KMS configured and answers the SSE header with an error rather than ignoring
# it, so defaulting it on would break evidence upload on a fresh checkout.
#
# PRODUCTION MUST SET THIS to AES256, *or* enable default encryption on the
# bucket itself (the better answer where available — it cannot be forgotten
# per-request). The server logs a loud warning at startup if neither is
# declared; see src/instrumentation.ts. Only the exact value "AES256" is
# honoured — anything else is ignored rather than sent, so a typo cannot
# fail every upload.
#
# S3_EVIDENCE_SSE=AES256
#
# Set this if the bucket already encrypts everything it stores, to say so and
# silence the startup warning. It changes no request; it is a declaration.
# S3_EVIDENCE_ENCRYPTED_AT_BUCKET=true

# Watermark previews (ugcportal-44q). Every uploaded image gets a downscaled,
# watermarked WebP preview stored under previews/ — that, and only that, is
# what browsing surfaces are allowed to show; the full-resolution original
# stays behind the paid-download flow (ugcportal-5d6). Videos get no preview
# yet (ugcportal-pmb) and are excluded from listings until they do.
# Optional. The text tiled diagonally across every preview. Defaults to
# "ugcportal"; set it to the studio/brand name you want stamped on previews.
# Keep it short — it's repeated across the whole frame and truncated at 40
# characters. Whitespace is collapsed and control characters are stripped, so
# an odd value can't break preview generation.
# WATERMARK_TEXT=ugcportal
#
# Drawing that text needs a font installed on the host: libvips ships none.
# The Docker image installs fontconfig + font-dejavu; macOS and most Linux
# desktops already have fonts. If none is found, image uploads fail with a
# 5xx rather than quietly producing unmarked previews.

# Preview concurrency (ugcportal-e86). Generating a preview allocates in
# native libvips memory — outside the V8 heap, so no Node flag bounds it and
# the only thing with an opinion about the total is the kernel's OOM killer.
# A burst of large uploads with nothing in the way is enough to take the
# container down, so at most N previews render at once, the next few callers
# queue, and anything past that is rejected with a retryable error rather
# than left to wait.
#
# All five of the WATERMARK_* variables below are optional, and the defaults
# are derived rather than guessed.
# The app reads the container's cgroup memory limit and spends it in order:
# 15% of the limit is set aside as headroom and never allocated; then 320 MB
# for the rest of the process; then 148 MB per in-flight upload (128 for the
# decode plus 20 for the request body it is decoding), up to a ceiling of one
# less than the libuv worker pool, so 3 by default; then whatever is left
# goes to the queue at 20 MB per waiting upload. Every upload the gate is
# holding, running or queued, costs 20 MB because the route keeps the body in
# memory twice for the whole request — so the queue is bounded in bytes
# rather than as a flat multiple of the limit.
#
# The headroom exists because the 128 MB figure was measured on macOS and has
# never been validated on the alpine image this ships in. Spending the budget
# to the last byte would turn any under-estimate into an OOM kill instead of
# the shedding this is all for. 15% at 1 GB is ~154 MB — more than one whole
# in-flight upload, which is the unit such errors come in.
#
#   memory limit | at once | queued | burst absorbed | largest upload
#   -------------+---------+--------+----------------+---------------
#        512 MB  |    -    |    -   |  does not fit  |     10 MB
#        768 MB  |    2    |    1   |       3        |    102 MB
#          1 GB  |    3    |    5   |       8        |    211 MB  <- recommended
#          2 GB  |    3    |   12   |      15        |    646 MB  (ceiling-bound)
#
# "Burst absorbed" counts maximum-size (10 MB) images. "Largest upload" is the
# biggest single file the container can buffer at all — 1 GB is the smallest
# size that fits a maximum-size (200 MB) video, which is refused with a 413
# below that.
#
# What an upload actually holds depends on whether it declares its length,
# and it is worth being exact because the difference is about 3x:
#
#  - with a Content-Length, which every browser form sends, the reservation
#    is twice the declared size, so an ordinary 3 MB photo holds about 6 MB
#    and a burst of them goes well past the "burst absorbed" column;
#  - without one, the reservation starts at a flat grant of one maximum-size
#    image (about 21 MB) and grows only as bytes arrive. For an image that
#    grant already covers the whole cap, so a 3 MB photo sent chunked holds
#    the full 21 MB from the moment it is admitted.
#
# So the column is a floor, not a forecast — but it is Content-Length that
# earns the slack, not the growing. Size burst capacity from the column
# unless you know your clients declare their lengths.
#
# A request that claims a 200 MB video and then stalls holds only the grant,
# whatever it claimed: nothing beyond it is committed until the bytes arrive.
#
# Two things about the upload-body budget are worth knowing operationally and
# need no configuration:
#  - a request body that goes 30 seconds without delivering a chunk is cut off
#    with a 408 and its reservation released, so one stalled client cannot
#    hold a share of the budget until Node's request timeout. It is an idle
#    timeout, so a slow-but-steady large upload is unaffected;
#  - a shed upload logs one throttled warning line naming what was wanted and
#    what was held ("[media] upload shed: ..."), at most one per 10 seconds
#    with the rest counted. That line is the signal to key a saturation alert
#    on; the 503s themselves are not logged, for the same reason the preview
#    gate's are not.
#
# Run the container with an explicit memory limit (`docker run --memory=1g`).
# With none set the app falls back to the host's total RAM, which on a shared
# machine over-provisions the limit. It warns when that happens, along with
# the configuration it chose and where each number came from — but on the
# first image upload, not at process start, because that is when preview
# generation is first configured.
#
# 1 GB is the recommended floor: it absorbs an ordinary multi-image
# selection with room over, where 768 MB takes three and sheds the fourth.
# 512 MB does not fit a single upload once headroom is accounted for, and
# warns that it does not rather than pretending — on the first image upload,
# same as above. Below 1 GB, expect bursts to shed.
#
# These figures price gated image preview generation ONLY. The rest of the
# upload route — video, which reaches no gate at all (no preview until
# ugcportal-pmb); bodies read in full and only then rejected for exceeding
# their kind's cap; and images the gate sheds, which had already been
# buffered by the time it decided to — is bounded separately, by the
# upload-body budget in src/lib/upload-memory.ts (ugcportal-05b). That budget
# is derived from the same container limit, needs no variables of its own,
# and is what the "burst absorbed" and "largest upload" columns above come
# from. Its projection, `projectedUploadPathPeakBytes`, is the figure to size
# a container from; the gate's `projectedGatedPeakBytes` covers a subset of
# it and the two must not be added together. See the Dockerfile for the full
# accounting, including what is still outside both.
#
# If the deployment cannot expose a cgroup memory limit, set the budget
# directly rather than guessing at the limit: this is the knob that makes
# every derived number correct, where WATERMARK_MAX_CONCURRENCY alone would
# leave the queue sized from the host's RAM.
# WATERMARK_MEMORY_BUDGET_MB=1024
#
# Above ~1 GB the binding constraint stops being memory and becomes libuv's
# worker pool: each in-flight preview occupies one worker for its duration,
# and one is left free for DNS (every preview is followed by an S3 upload)
# and async file I/O. To use a bigger container, raise UV_THREADPOOL_SIZE.
#
# Do NOT set UV_THREADPOOL_SIZE in this file. libuv sizes its pool early in
# process start, and Next loads .env into process.env after that, so the
# value would be ignored by libuv but still read by the app — lifting its
# concurrency ceiling while the real pool stayed at 4, which is worse than
# not setting it at all. Set it in the container/orchestrator environment
# (docker run -e, compose `environment:`, k8s `env:`) where the process
# inherits it at exec time.
#
# Override the derived limit. Use this when the derivation itself is wrong;
# use WATERMARK_MEMORY_BUDGET_MB above when it is the budget that is wrong.
# Clamped to one less than the libuv worker pool (3 by default) — more
# previews than there are workers do not run, they queue invisibly inside
# libuv. A clamp is logged as a warning rather than applied silently.
# WATERMARK_MAX_CONCURRENCY=3
#
# How many uploads may wait for a slot. Defaults to whatever the memory
# budget affords, capped at 4x the limit. Set it to 0 for pure load
# shedding: reject immediately whenever all slots are busy. Setting it by
# hand opts out of the byte accounting, so budget ~20 MB per queued upload
# yourself.
# WATERMARK_QUEUE_LIMIT=12
#
# How long a queued upload may wait before it is rejected, in milliseconds.
# Defaults to 10000, comfortably inside a typical proxy or browser upload
# timeout so an overloaded server answers rather than having the connection
# cut from the other end with nothing logged. Clamped to 120000: past that
# the caller is gone and the connection is being held for nobody.
# WATERMARK_QUEUE_TIMEOUT_MS=10000
#
# libvips threads *per preview*. Defaults to effective-CPUs/limit, clamped
# to 4, which keeps total threads around one per CPU instead of one per CPU
# per in-flight image. Raising it makes a single upload on an idle server
# slightly faster and every concurrent burst use measurably more memory —
# which is why the clamp applies to an explicit value too: past 4 the
# measured per-operation memory figure this whole budget rests on stops
# describing reality.
# WATERMARK_SHARP_THREADS=1

# Instagram connect (ugcportal-5ce). Uses "Instagram API with Instagram
# Login": create an Instagram app at developers.facebook.com and register
# http://localhost:3000/api/admin/instagram/callback as an OAuth redirect URI.
# Only admins (User.role = ADMIN) can reach the connect flow — see
# ADMIN_BOOTSTRAP_EMAILS above for how to get your first one.
INSTAGRAM_CLIENT_ID=
INSTAGRAM_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Optional. Defaults to AUTH_URL + /api/admin/instagram/callback; set it
# explicitly only if the registered redirect URI differs from that.
# INSTAGRAM_REDIRECT_URI=
# Optional. Defaults to the least-privilege scope the sync job needs.
# INSTAGRAM_SCOPES=instagram_business_basic
# Encrypts connected-account access tokens at rest (AES-256-GCM).
# Generate with `openssl rand -base64 32`. Rotating this invalidates every
# stored token, and each account has to be reconnected.
INSTAGRAM_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# The contact address the About and Portfolio pages' mailto form sends to
# (ugcportal-qnq9.7). Left unset for local dev on purpose: every environment
# without it falls back to a clearly-fake placeholder on the `.invalid` TLD
# (src/lib/contact.ts). PRODUCTION MUST SET THIS to a real inbox, and to a
# BARE address only — no display name ("Name <addr@example.com>" is
# rejected, not silently parsed). Unlike the sign-in variables above, this
# does not fail the build or refuse a request: the server logs a loud
# warning at startup if it is unset or malformed in production (see
# checkContactEmailConfiguration in src/instrumentation.ts), and the
# placeholder address keeps rendering to visitors until it is fixed.
# CONTACT_EMAIL=

# Who is behind the site, for the privacy statement at /privacy and the
# licence text at /licence (ugcportal-qnq9.4). Read at request time by
# src/lib/legal/contact.ts; nothing else uses them.
#
# PRODUCTION MUST SET ALL FOUR. While any is unset, the two pages render the
# variable's name in square brackets outside production, and in production
# they refuse to render at all (Next serves its error page) — a privacy
# statement with no named controller does not satisfy GDPR Art. 13 and is
# worse than no page, because it looks like compliance. The server says which
# are missing at startup (src/instrumentation.ts).
#
# Environment variables rather than source so the controller's name — a
# private person's — stays out of a public repository, and because the
# providers are deployment facts src/lib/s3.ts is deliberately agnostic
# about. Why each exists, by GDPR Art. 13(1) item:
#
#  (a) the identity and contact details of the controller — the person the
#      site and its accounts are registered to (docs/ugc-research.md,
#      decisions table). The address is also where data-subject requests,
#      licence requests and objections go (Art. 12).
# LEGAL_CONTROLLER_NAME=
# LEGAL_CONTACT_EMAIL=
#
#  (e) recipients, and (f) whether personal data leaves the EEA: the company
#      that runs the server (sees every visitor's IP address, holds the
#      database) and the S3-compatible object store that holds uploads and
#      rights evidence. Give the legal name and the country the data sits in,
#      e.g. "Example Hosting AS, Norway".
# LEGAL_HOSTING_PROVIDER=
# LEGAL_STORAGE_PROVIDER=
#
# The pages also stay marked as a draft (visible notice, a
# `ugcportal:draft` meta tag, noindex) until the text is signed off under
# ugcportal-alg; that is LEGAL_SIGN_OFF in src/lib/legal/contact.ts, a fact
# about the text rather than the deployment, and is not configured here.

# Cookie consent gate (ugcportal-3wgp). Both unset today — Umami itself is
# deployed by ugcportal-8at and wired into page tracking by ugcportal-9w0,
# neither of which has landed yet. src/components/consent/analytics-loader.tsx
# is the ONLY place either of these is read; it renders nothing at all while
# they are unset, and only once a visitor has actively granted optional
# cookies (the banner in src/components/consent/cookie-banner.tsx) once they
# are set.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_UMAMI_SRC=https://your-umami-host/script.js
# NEXT_PUBLIC_UMAMI_WEBSITE_ID=

# Search Console and Pinterest site verification (ugcportal-qnq9.12, K4),
# read by src/lib/site-verification.ts and rendered as plain <meta> tags in
# the root layout (src/app/layout.tsx) — never a script. Both optional and
# independent: leaving one or both unset simply omits that tag, it does not
# block anything from rendering (unlike the LEGAL_* variables above).
#
#   GOOGLE_SITE_VERIFICATION    the code from Search Console's HTML-tag
#                               verification method (NOT the HTML-file
#                               method, which this app does not serve).
#   PINTEREST_SITE_VERIFICATION the code from Pinterest's "Claim your
#                               website" meta-tag method.
# GOOGLE_SITE_VERIFICATION=
# PINTEREST_SITE_VERIFICATION=

# The public origin the sitemap (/sitemap.xml) and robots.txt build
# fully-qualified URLs from (ugcportal-qnq9.12), and the one a per-item
# page's canonical link uses. Reuses AUTH_URL (see src/lib/origin.ts's
# siteOrigin()) rather than a second "what is our domain" variable — set
# AUTH_URL for production and both follow.
